const USFM = `\\id GAL - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\\h Galatians
\\toc1 The Letter of Paul to the Assemblies in Galatia
\\toc2 Galatians
\\toc3 Gal
\\mt1 Galatians

\\imt Introduction
\\ip This letter was written by Paul to multiple assemblies of believers across the Roman province of Galatia (in modern-day central Turkey), likely around 48-55 AD. The recipients were predominantly Gentile converts from paganism who had received the good news from Paul directly and who had visibly received the Spirit of God through faith — not through any legal process.
\\ip After Paul's departure, rival teachers arrived and began persuading these Gentile believers that their faith in the Christ was not enough — that they must formally convert to Judaism through circumcision and take on the full obligations of the Sinai covenant in order to have standing before God.
\\ip Paul writes with unusual urgency. Unlike every other letter bearing his name, this one contains no word of thanksgiving for the recipients. Where praise would normally appear, Paul instead expresses shock that the Galatians are already in the process of abandoning the gospel he delivered to them. The letter is simultaneously a defense of Paul's authority as an apostle (received directly from God, not through any human chain), a defense of his gospel (that Gentiles have full standing before God through faithfulness to the Christ, without becoming Jewish), and a warning that accepting circumcision as a requirement would mean abandoning the grace of the Christ entirely.

\\c 1
\\s1 The Greeting as Argument
\\p
\\v 1 Paul, an apostle \\add [i.e. one who is sent with the full authority of the sender]\\add* — not from men \\add [i.e. my commission did not originate from any human council]\\add* nor through a man \\add [i.e. nor was it transmitted to me through any human intermediary]\\add*, but through Jesus the Christ and God the Father, who raised him from the dead \\add [and thereby established his authority as Lord]\\add* —
\\v 2 and all the brothers who are with me \\add [they stand behind this message, but this letter is mine alone]\\add*, to the assemblies \\add [of believers]\\add* in Galatia:
\\p
\\v 3 Grace \\add [i.e. God's favor, given through the merit of his Son]\\add* to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus the Christ,
\\v 4 who gave himself \\add [as a sacrifice]\\add* for our sins so that he might deliver us \\add [as God once delivered Israel from Egypt]\\add* from the present evil age\\f + \\fr 1:4 \\ft See Daniel 12:1-2 for the Jewish two-age framework.\\f* \\add [i.e. this current era of sin and death, before the age to come]\\add*, according to the will of our God and Father \\add [i.e. this rescue was not an afterthought — it was God's plan]\\add*,
\\v 5 to whom be the glory forever and ever. Amen.

\\s1 The Rebuke and Anathema
\\p
\\v 6 I am astonished \\add [— where I would normally give thanks for you, I cannot —]\\add* that you are so quickly deserting the one who called you \\add [i.e. God himself]\\add* in the grace of the Christ and are turning to a different \\add [kind of]\\add* gospel \\add [i.e. the demand that you must become Jewish through circumcision to have standing before God]\\add* —
\\v 7 not that there is another \\add [legitimate]\\add* gospel, but there are some \\add [teachers who arrived after I left]\\add* who are troubling you and want to distort \\add [i.e. reverse the very meaning of]\\add* the gospel of the Christ.
\\v 8 But even if we \\add [i.e. I myself or any of my colleagues]\\add* or an angel from heaven should proclaim to you a gospel contrary to the one we proclaimed to you, let him be accursed\\f + \\fr 1:8 \\ft Greek anathema, from Hebrew cherem, the irrevocable ban of total destruction (Leviticus 27:28-29).\\f* \\add [i.e. under the ban of God, devoted to destruction]\\add*.
\\v 9 As we have said before \\add [when I was with you]\\add*, so now I say again: If anyone is proclaiming to you a gospel contrary to the one you received \\add [from me]\\add*, let him be accursed.
\\p
\\v 10 For am I now seeking the approval of men, or of God? Or am I trying to please men? \\add [Does the man who just pronounced the ban of God sound like a people-pleaser?]\\add* If I were still \\add [as in my former life]\\add* trying to please men, I would not be a servant of the Christ.

\\s1 Paul's Gospel Received by Revelation
\\p
\\v 11 For I would have you know, brothers, that the gospel proclaimed by me is not of human origin \\add [i.e. it was not designed by men or derived from human tradition]\\add*.
\\v 12 For I did not receive it from any man, nor was I taught it \\add [by any human teacher — not even the apostles in Jerusalem]\\add*, but I received it through a direct revelation of Jesus the Christ.
\\p
\\v 13 For you have heard of my former way of life in Judaism \\add [i.e. my zealous commitment to the Jewish way of life and ancestral customs — not merely my ethnicity]\\add*, how I persecuted the assembly of God beyond all measure and tried to destroy it \\add [through a sustained campaign of violence]\\add*.
\\v 14 And I was advancing in Judaism beyond many of my own age among my people, being far more zealous for the traditions of my fathers \\add [i.e. the whole body of ancestral customs and oral interpretations built upon the written Torah — the very same traditions these rival teachers are now urging you to adopt]\\add*.

\\s1 Called from the Womb, Independent from Jerusalem
\\p
\\v 15 But when God, who had set me apart from my mother's womb\\f + \\fr 1:15 \\ft Compare Jeremiah 1:5 and Isaiah 49:1.\\f* \\add [just as he set apart Jeremiah and the Servant of Isaiah — appointed before birth for a mission to the nations]\\add* and called me through his grace \\add [i.e. his unmerited favor — not because of my qualifications, which were qualifications for persecution]\\add*,
\\v 16 was pleased to reveal his Son in me \\add [i.e. both to me and through me]\\add*, so that I might proclaim him among the Gentiles \\add [— this was the purpose of my calling from before I was born —]\\add*, I did not immediately consult with flesh and blood \\add [i.e. any human being]\\add*,
\\v 17 nor did I go up to Jerusalem to those who were apostles before me \\add [i.e. I did not seek training or authorization from the senior apostles]\\add*, but I went away into Arabia \\add [i.e. the Nabatean kingdom, east of Damascus]\\add*, and returned again to Damascus \\add [where my encounter with the risen Christ had taken place]\\add*.
\\p
\\v 18 Then after three years \\add [of proclaiming the gospel without any contact with the Jerusalem leadership]\\add* I went up to Jerusalem to become acquainted with Cephas\\f + \\fr 1:18 \\ft Greek historesai: to visit for the purpose of getting to know someone, not to receive instruction.\\f* \\add [i.e. Peter — to get to know him, not to receive instruction from him]\\add* and remained with him \\add [only]\\add* fifteen days.
\\v 19 But I saw none of the other apostles except James the brother of the Lord \\add [i.e. Jesus' own brother, who had become a leader of the Jerusalem assembly]\\add*.
\\v 20 In what I am writing to you — \\add [I swear]\\add* before God — I do not lie!
\\p
\\v 21 Then I went \\add [not back to Jerusalem, but away]\\add* into the regions of Syria \\add [whose chief city, Antioch, became my base of operations]\\add* and Cilicia \\add [my home region, whose chief city was Tarsus]\\add*.
\\v 22 And I was still unknown by face to the assemblies of Judea \\add [i.e. the communities nearest to the Jerusalem apostles]\\add* that are in the Christ.
\\v 23 They only kept hearing, "He who once persecuted us is now proclaiming the faith \\add [i.e. the message and convictions of our movement]\\add* he once tried to destroy."
\\v 24 And they glorified God because of me \\add [— they did not summon me, did not interrogate me, did not demand I present myself for authorization. They recognized that my transformation was God's work, and they praised him for it]\\add*.

\\c 2
\\s1 The Jerusalem Leaders Add Nothing
\\p
\\v 1 Then after fourteen years \\add [of carrying out my mission among the Gentiles without a second visit to Jerusalem]\\add* I went up again to Jerusalem with Barnabas, taking Titus along with me \\add [— an uncircumcised Greek, brought deliberately as a test case]\\add*.
\\v 2 I went up because of a revelation \\add [i.e. God directed me to go — I was not summoned by the Jerusalem leaders]\\add* and set before them the gospel that I proclaim among the Gentiles \\add [— not submitting it for correction, but presenting what I was already preaching]\\add* — though privately before those who seemed influential — so that I would not be running or had not run in vain \\add [i.e. so that a public rupture with Jerusalem would not undermine my Gentile mission — not because I doubted my gospel]\\add*.
\\v 3 But even Titus, who was with me, was not compelled to be circumcised, though he was a Greek \\add [— the Jerusalem leaders themselves did not require it]\\add*.
\\v 4 Yet \\add [the matter came up]\\add* because of false brothers secretly brought in — who slipped in to spy out our freedom \\add [from the requirement of proselyte conversion]\\add* that we have in the Christ Jesus, so that they might bring us into slavery \\add [i.e. bondage under the demand that Gentiles must become Jewish to have standing before God]\\add* —
\\v 5 to them we did not yield in submission even for a moment, so that the truth of the gospel might be preserved for you \\add [— what I fought for in Jerusalem is the very gospel you received]\\add*.
\\p
\\v 6 And from those who seemed to be influential \\add [i.e. the recognized leaders]\\add* — what they were \\add [in terms of status]\\add* makes no difference to me; God shows no partiality — those, I say, who seemed influential added nothing to me \\add [i.e. they did not supplement, correct, or alter my gospel in any way]\\add*.
\\v 7 On the contrary, when they saw that I had been entrusted with the gospel to the uncircumcised \\add [i.e. the Gentiles]\\add*, just as Peter \\add [had been entrusted with the gospel]\\add* to the circumcised \\add [i.e. the Jews]\\add* —
\\v 8 for he who worked through Peter for his apostolic ministry to the circumcised worked also through me for mine to the Gentiles \\add [— the same God, the same power, two audiences]\\add* —
\\v 9 and when James and Cephas \\add [i.e. Peter]\\add* and John, who seemed to be pillars \\add [i.e. the structural supports of the Jerusalem community]\\add*, perceived the grace that was given to me, they gave the right hand of fellowship \\add [a formal gesture of alliance and mutual recognition]\\add* to Barnabas and me, agreeing that we should go to the Gentiles and they to the circumcised.
\\v 10 Only \\add [— and this was their sole request —]\\add*, they asked us to remember the poor\\f + \\fr 2:10 \\ft This collection became a major theme; see Romans 15:25-28; 1 Corinthians 16:1-4; 2 Corinthians 8-9.\\f* \\add [in the Jerusalem community]\\add*, the very thing I was eager to do.

\\s1 Paul Confronts Peter at Antioch
\\p
\\v 11 But when Cephas \\add [i.e. Peter]\\add* came to Antioch \\add [the third largest city in the Roman empire and my base of operations]\\add*, I opposed him to his face, because he stood condemned \\add [by his own contradictory behavior]\\add*.
\\v 12 For before certain men came from James \\add [the same James who had extended the right hand of fellowship]\\add*, he was eating with the Gentiles \\add [— sharing meals with uncircumcised believers, thereby demonstrating that proselyte conversion was not required for full community membership]\\add*; but when they came he drew back and separated himself, fearing the circumcision party \\add [— not out of conviction, but out of cowardice]\\add*.
\\v 13 And the rest of the Jews \\add [in the Antioch assembly]\\add* acted hypocritically along with him \\add [— their behavior did not match their actual convictions]\\add*, so that even Barnabas \\add [my own missionary partner, who had stood with me in Jerusalem]\\add* was led astray by their hypocrisy.
\\v 14 But when I saw that their conduct was not in step with the truth of the gospel, I said to Cephas before them all, "If you, though a Jew, live like a Gentile \\add [by eating with uncircumcised believers]\\add* and not like a Jew, how can you force the Gentiles to live like Jews \\add [— your withdrawal tells them they must convert to Judaism to be accepted]\\add*?"

\\s1 Justified by Faith, Not by Works of the Law
\\p
\\v 15 \\add [I continued:]\\add* We ourselves are Jews by birth and not Gentile sinners \\add [— that is, we had every advantage of being inside the covenant]\\add*;
\\v 16 yet we know that a person is not justified \\add [i.e. declared righteous by God]\\add* by works of the law \\add [i.e. by circumcision and entry into the Sinai covenant]\\add* but through the faithfulness of Jesus the Christ\\f + \\fr 2:16 \\ft The Greek pistis Christou can mean "faith in Christ" or "the faithfulness of Christ." Both readings are grammatically possible; see also Romans 3:22.\\f* — so we also have put our trust in the Christ Jesus, in order to be justified by the faithfulness of the Christ and not by works of the law, because by works of the law no one \\add [— no flesh at all —]\\add* will be justified.
\\v 17 But if, in seeking to be justified in the Christ, we too were found to be sinners \\add [by the law's own standards, because we abandoned it as the basis of our standing]\\add*, is the Christ then a servant of sin? By no means!
\\v 18 For if I rebuild what I tore down \\add [i.e. if I reimpose the law-covenant system as the basis of justification after having recognized its insufficiency]\\add*, I prove myself to be a transgressor \\add [— the transgression is not in leaving the law, but in returning to it after having recognized that it cannot justify]\\add*.
\\v 19 For through the law I died to the law \\add [— the law's own curse, executed on the Christ, exhausted its claim on me]\\add*, so that I might live to God. I have been crucified with the Christ\\f + \\fr 2:19 \\ft Compare Romans 6:6 and Romans 7:4.\\f* \\add [— I died when he died]\\add*.
\\v 20 It is no longer I who live, but the Christ who lives in me. And the life I now live in the body I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave himself for me.
\\v 21 I do not nullify the grace of God \\add [— but those who rebuild the law-system as a basis of justification do]\\add*, for if righteousness were through the law, then the Christ died for no purpose \\add [— and that is the one conclusion no one can accept]\\add*.

\\c 3
\\s1 The Appeal to Experience
\\p
\\v 1 O foolish Galatians! Who has bewitched you \\add [— who has cast a spell over your minds, that you can no longer see what is right in front of you?]\\add* It was before your eyes that Jesus the Christ was publicly displayed as crucified \\add [— I proclaimed his death so vividly that it was as though the cross itself had been posted in your town square]\\add*.
\\v 2 This only I want to learn from you: Did you receive the Spirit by works of the law \\add [i.e. by first becoming Jewish through circumcision]\\add* or by hearing with faith \\add [i.e. by hearing the gospel message and trusting it]\\add*?
\\v 3 Are you so foolish? Having begun by the Spirit \\add [— whose arrival among you was undeniable and powerful —]\\add*, are you now being perfected by the flesh \\add [i.e. by the knife of circumcision — as though a surgical procedure could complete what the Spirit of God began]\\add*?
\\v 4 Did you experience so many things in vain — if indeed it was in vain \\add [— I hold out hope that you have not yet crossed the point of no return]\\add*?
\\v 5 Does he who supplies the Spirit to you \\add [— God, who has been generously and continuously pouring out his Spirit among you —]\\add* and works miracles among you do so by works of the law, or by hearing with faith \\add [— you know the answer: the Spirit came through faith, not through circumcision, and the miracles you have witnessed confirm it]\\add*?

\\s1 Abraham — Faith, Not Circumcision
\\p
\\v 6 Just as Abraham "believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness"\\f + \\fr 3:6 \\ft Genesis 15:6. This declaration occurred before circumcision was instituted in Genesis 17.\\f* \\add [— and notice: this declaration was made in Genesis 15, before circumcision even existed as a covenant sign]\\add* —
\\v 7 know then that those who are of faith \\add [i.e. those whose standing before God rests on trust, not on circumcision]\\add* — these are the sons of Abraham \\add [— sonship is determined by sharing the father's defining act, which was faith, not a mark on the body]\\add*.
\\v 8 And the Scripture, foreseeing that God would justify the Gentiles by faith, proclaimed the gospel beforehand to Abraham, saying, "In you shall all the nations be blessed"\\f + \\fr 3:8 \\ft Genesis 12:3; compare 18:18.\\f* \\add [— this promise was made in Genesis 12, centuries before the Sinai covenant existed; the blessing of the nations was always part of the Abrahamic promise, not an afterthought]\\add*.
\\v 9 So then, those who are of faith are blessed along with Abraham, the man of faith \\add [— you share his faith, therefore you share his blessing, without needing to share his circumcision]\\add*.

\\s1 The Curse of the Law and the Redemption of the Christ
\\p
\\v 10 For all who rely on works of the law \\add [i.e. all whose standing before God depends on having entered the Sinai covenant through circumcision]\\add* are under a curse; for it is written, "Cursed is everyone who does not abide by all the things written in the book of the law, and do them"\\f + \\fr 3:10 \\ft Deuteronomy 27:26.\\f* \\add [— the covenant's own terms demand comprehensive obedience; any failure activates the curse]\\add*.
\\v 11 Now it is evident that no one is justified before God by the law, for "The righteous one shall live by faith"\\f + \\fr 3:11 \\ft Habakkuk 2:4.\\f* \\add [— the prophet Habakkuk declared that life comes through trust, not through the law-covenant system]\\add*.
\\v 12 But the law is not of faith; rather, "The one who does them shall live by them"\\f + \\fr 3:12 \\ft Leviticus 18:5.\\f* \\add [— the law's operating principle is performance: do and live. Faith's operating principle is trust: believe and live. These are two different systems, and they cannot both serve as the ground of one's standing before God]\\add*.
\\v 13 The Christ redeemed us \\add [— us who were Jews, held under the law's jurisdiction]\\add* from the curse of the law by becoming a curse for us \\add [— he did not merely bear a curse; he became the curse itself]\\add* — for it is written, "Cursed is everyone who is hanged on a tree"\\f + \\fr 3:13 \\ft Deuteronomy 21:23.\\f* \\add [— the law of Moses declared that the crucified are accursed; when the Christ was crucified, the law's own curse fell on him, and its claim on those united with him was exhausted]\\add* —
\\v 14 so that in the Christ Jesus the blessing of Abraham might come to the Gentiles \\add [— the curse that held the covenant people was the obstacle; with the curse spent on the Christ, the promised blessing now flows freely to the nations]\\add*, so that we \\add [— all of us, Jew and Gentile together —]\\add* might receive the promise of the Spirit through faith.

\\s1 The Promise Cannot Be Annulled
\\p
\\v 15 Brothers, I speak in human terms \\add [— let me use an everyday example]\\add*: Even with a man-made covenant, once it has been ratified, no one annuls it or adds conditions to it \\add [— if this is true of human agreements, how much more of a divine one?]\\add*.
\\v 16 Now the promises were spoken to Abraham and to his offspring. It does not say, "And to offsprings," as though referring to many, but referring to one, "And to your offspring"\\f + \\fr 3:16 \\ft See Genesis 13:15; 17:8; 22:18.\\f* \\add [— the singular form is deliberate]\\add* — who is the Christ.
\\v 17 This is what I mean: the law, which came 430 years afterward\\f + \\fr 3:17 \\ft The 430-year figure follows Exodus 12:40 (LXX).\\f* \\add [i.e. the Sinai covenant arrived centuries after the Abrahamic promise was already in force]\\add*, does not annul a covenant previously ratified by God, so as to make the promise void \\add [— a later addition cannot rewrite an earlier agreement]\\add*.
\\v 18 For if the inheritance comes by the law, it no longer comes by promise; but God graciously gave it to Abraham by a promise \\add [— and these two bases are mutually exclusive: it is either a gift by promise or a wage by law-keeping, not both]\\add*.

\\s1 The Purpose of the Law
\\p
\\v 19 Why then the law \\add [— if the promise is sufficient and the law cannot alter it, what was the law for]\\add*? It was added because of transgressions \\add [i.e. to make sin identifiable as specific violations of specific commands]\\add*, until the offspring should come to whom the promise had been made \\add [— the law's role had a built-in expiration: it lasted until the Christ, the promised offspring, arrived]\\add*, and it was put in place through angels by the hand of a mediator\\f + \\fr 3:19 \\ft The tradition that angels mediated the giving of the law appears also in Acts 7:53 and Hebrews 2:2.\\f* \\add [i.e. Moses, who stood between God and Israel at Sinai — unlike the Abrahamic promise, which God gave directly]\\add*.
\\v 20 Now a mediator implies more than one party, but God is one \\add [— the law required a go-between because it was a bilateral agreement between two parties; the Abrahamic promise required none, because God made it unilaterally — he alone passed between the pieces of the covenant sacrifice while Abraham slept]\\add*.
\\v 21 Is the law then contrary to the promises of God? By no means! For if a law had been given that could give life \\add [— but no such law exists]\\add*, then righteousness would indeed be by the law \\add [— the law is not God's enemy; it simply cannot do what the promise does]\\add*.
\\v 22 But the Scripture imprisoned everything under sin \\add [— the Torah's own testimony about human failure confined all people, without exception, under sin's power]\\add*, so that the promise by faith in Jesus the Christ might be given to those who believe \\add [— the imprisonment served the promise: by eliminating every alternative, it directed everything toward faith as the only way out]\\add*.

\\s1 The Guardian's Role Has Ended
\\p
\\v 23 Now before faith came \\add [i.e. before the faith-based era was inaugurated by the Christ's arrival]\\add*, we \\add [— we who are Jews —]\\add* were held captive under the law, imprisoned until the coming faith would be revealed.
\\v 24 So then, the law was our guardian \\add [— like the household slave in a Greek or Roman family who escorted a child to and from school, supervised his conduct, and enforced discipline — a role that was real and necessary, but not permanent, and not the same as being the teacher]\\add* until the Christ came, in order that we might be justified by faith.
\\v 25 But now that faith has come, we are no longer under a guardian \\add [— the guardian's commission has expired; and if even those who were under the guardian no longer need one, how absurd for you — who were never under the guardian at all — to place yourselves under him now]\\add*.

\\s1 Sons of God, Abraham's Offspring
\\p
\\v 26 For in the Christ Jesus you \\add [— all of you, Gentile believers who never underwent circumcision —]\\add* are all sons of God, through faith.
\\v 27 For as many of you as were baptized into the Christ have put on the Christ\\f + \\fr 3:27 \\ft Compare Romans 6:3-4.\\f* \\add [— your baptism was the moment you were clothed in a new identity; your public standing is now defined by the Christ, not by your former markers — ethnic, social, or otherwise]\\add*.
\\v 28 There is neither Jew nor Greek \\add [— the very boundary the rival teachers are trying to reimpose]\\add*, there is neither slave nor free, there is no male and female\\f + \\fr 3:28 \\ft The phrase "male and female" echoes Genesis 1:27, suggesting that even creation-order distinctions do not determine standing in Christ.\\f* \\add [— even the distinction that made circumcision a males-only entry requirement is irrelevant]\\add*, for you are all one in the Christ Jesus.
\\v 29 And if you belong to the Christ, then you are Abraham's offspring, heirs according to promise \\add [— everything the rival teachers say you need circumcision to become, you already are: Abraham's seed, holders of the inheritance, recipients of the blessing. Circumcision cannot add you to a family you already belong to]\\add*.

\\c 4
\\s1 The Heir Who Lived as a Slave
\\p
\\v 1 I mean that the heir, as long as he is a child \\add [i.e. a minor who has not yet reached the age his father set for him to take control]\\add*, is no different from a slave, though he is the owner of everything \\add [— he holds the title but cannot exercise it]\\add*,
\\v 2 but he is under guardians and managers until the date set by his father \\add [— the father, not the guardians, decides when their authority ends]\\add*.
\\v 3 In the same way we also \\add [— we who are Jews]\\add*, when we were children \\add [i.e. during the era before the Christ]\\add*, were enslaved under the elementary principles of the world \\add [i.e. under the regulatory system of the Sinai covenant — its calendar, its food laws, its rituals — not because these were evil, but because living under them as the condition of standing before God was a form of bondage]\\add*.
\\v 4 But when the fullness of time had come \\add [— the exact date the Father had set from the beginning —]\\add*, God sent forth his Son, born of a woman\\f + \\fr 4:4 \\ft "Born of a woman" is a Hebrew idiom for ordinary humanity (Job 14:1; 15:14; 25:4). Compare John 1:14; Romans 8:3.\\f* \\add [i.e. fully human, entering our condition without reservation]\\add*, born under the law \\add [i.e. born a Jew, within the Sinai covenant's jurisdiction, subject to all its obligations]\\add*,
\\v 5 to redeem those who were under the law \\add [— to buy them out of the covenant system that held them, as God once bought Israel out of slavery in Egypt]\\add*, so that we might receive adoption as sons \\add [— the formal, legally recognized status of full children with full inheritance rights — and this "we" now includes all believers, Jew and Gentile]\\add*.
\\v 6 And because you are sons \\add [— notice: you already are sons; the Spirit does not make you sons but confirms what you already are —]\\add*, God has sent the Spirit of his Son into our hearts, crying, "Abba! Father!"\\f + \\fr 4:6 \\ft See Romans 8:15-16; Mark 14:36.\\f* \\add [— "Abba" is the Aramaic term for father used in Jewish family life, the same word Jesus himself used in prayer; the Spirit of God's own Son is within you, addressing God with the intimacy of a child speaking to a parent]\\add*
\\v 7 So you are no longer a slave, but a son, and if a son, then an heir through God \\add [— the minor heir of verse 1 has come of age; the guardians' authority has ended; the inheritance is yours, given by God's own initiative]\\add*.

\\s1 How Can You Turn Back?
\\p
\\v 8 Formerly, when you did not know God, you were enslaved to those that by nature are not gods \\add [i.e. the pagan deities, cosmic forces, and idols you worshiped before your conversion — entities that are not genuinely divine]\\add*.
\\v 9 But now that you have come to know God — or rather, to be known by God \\add [— the more precise truth is that God chose you first; the relationship rests on his initiative, not on your religious performance]\\add* — how can you turn back again to the weak and worthless elementary principles \\add [i.e. the same category of bondage you just escaped — "again" is the devastating word here: you were never under the Torah before, yet adopting it now as the basis of your standing before God is a return to the same structural position, because both your old paganism and this new demand function identically — both condition access to God on compliance with an external regulatory system]\\add*? Do you wish to be enslaved by them all over again?
\\v 10 You observe days and months and seasons and years \\add [— you have already begun keeping the Jewish liturgical calendar: Sabbaths, new moons, the annual festivals, perhaps even sabbatical years — the boundary markers of covenant identity that signal you are moving toward full proselyte conversion]\\add*!
\\v 11 I am afraid I may have labored over you in vain \\add [— not because the gospel I preached was deficient, but because you are abandoning it for a system that cannot deliver what it promises]\\add*.

\\s1 Remember How You Received Me
\\p
\\v 12 Brothers, I entreat you, become as I am, for I also became as you are \\add [— I, a Jew, set aside the law's covenant jurisdiction as the basis of standing before God and accepted you as full members without requiring you to become Jewish; now I ask you to remain in that same freedom rather than entering the system I left behind]\\add*. You did me no wrong \\add [— our relationship was without conflict; all this tension is the product of the rival teachers' interference]\\add*.
\\v 13 You know it was because of a bodily ailment that I preached the gospel to you at first \\add [— my visit to Galatia was unplanned; an illness forced me to stop, and in that weakness I proclaimed the message]\\add*,
\\v 14 and though my condition was a trial to you \\add [— in the ancient world, a suffering teacher could be seen as cursed by the gods; you had every cultural reason to reject me]\\add*, you did not scorn or despise me, but received me as an angel of God, as the Christ Jesus \\add [— and now you are turning away from the message of the man you once welcomed as though he were the Christ himself]\\add*.
\\v 15 What then has become of your joy? For I testify to you that, if possible, you would have gouged out your eyes and given them to me \\add [— that is how much you loved me then; what happened?]\\add*.
\\v 16 Have I then become your enemy by telling you the truth?
\\p
\\v 17 They \\add [i.e. the rival teachers]\\add* make much of you, but for no good purpose. They want to shut you out \\add [— to make you feel excluded from full standing before God]\\add*, so that you will make much of them \\add [— so that you will depend on them as the gatekeepers of your acceptance]\\add*.
\\v 18 It is always good to be courted with good intentions, and not only when I am present with you \\add [— genuine care does not require the teacher's physical presence; manipulation works best when the honest teacher is absent]\\add*.
\\v 19 My little children, for whom I am again in the anguish of childbirth \\add [— I already went through the pain of bringing you to birth in the Christ during my first visit; the rival teachers have undone that birth, and now I labor again]\\add* until the Christ is formed in you \\add [— my goal is not outward compliance with regulations but the inward shaping of your lives by the Christ himself]\\add*!
\\v 20 I wish I could be present with you now and change my tone \\add [— a letter has only one register; if I were with you, I could respond to your faces, soften where needed, press where needed]\\add*, for I am perplexed about you \\add [— I do not know what will reach you]\\add*.

\\s1 The Two Sons, the Two Covenants
\\p
\\v 21 Tell me, you who desire to be under the law \\add [i.e. you who want to enter the Sinai covenant through circumcision]\\add*: do you not listen to what the law actually says?
\\v 22 For it is written that Abraham had two sons, one by a slave woman \\add [Hagar, an Egyptian servant]\\add* and one by a free woman \\add [Sarah, his wife]\\add*.\\f + \\fr 4:22 \\ft The full narrative spans Genesis 16-21.\\f*
\\v 23 But the son of the slave was born according to the flesh \\add [i.e. through human initiative — Abraham and Sarah's plan to produce an heir by their own efforts when the promise seemed to have failed]\\add*, while the son of the free woman was born through promise \\add [i.e. through God's direct intervention — Sarah was barren and old; Isaac's birth required divine power, not human planning]\\add*.
\\v 24 Now this may be interpreted as an allegory \\add [i.e. the narrative carries a meaning beyond its surface]\\add*: these women are two covenants. One is from Mount Sinai, bearing children for slavery; she is Hagar \\add [— the Sinai covenant, when used as the mechanism for producing Abraham's children through circumcision, produces slaves, not free children]\\add*.
\\v 25 Now Hagar corresponds to Mount Sinai in Arabia \\add [— both are connected geographically and theologically]\\add*; she corresponds to the present Jerusalem \\add [i.e. the earthly city and its temple-centered religious establishment, the base from which the rival teachers operate]\\add*, for she is in slavery with her children \\add [— the system of proselyte conversion that the present Jerusalem represents produces bondage, not freedom]\\add*.
\\v 26 But the Jerusalem above is free, and she is our mother \\add [— the heavenly city that represents God's ultimate purpose, the reality toward which history moves; she is the mother of all who belong to the Christ, Jew and Gentile alike]\\add*.
\\v 27 For it is written, "Rejoice, O barren one who does not bear; break forth and cry aloud, you who are not in labor! For the children of the desolate one will be more than those of the one who has a husband"\\f + \\fr 4:27 \\ft Isaiah 54:1.\\f* \\add [— Isaiah spoke of barren Jerusalem's future abundance; Paul reads this as a prophecy of the promise-method: it appears barren because it does not produce children through circumcision, yet its children — those born through faith — will outnumber those produced by the flesh-method]\\add*.
\\p
\\v 28 Now you, brothers, like Isaac, are children of promise \\add [— you entered Abraham's family the same way Isaac did: not through human effort but through God's promise and power]\\add*.
\\v 29 But just as at that time he who was born according to the flesh persecuted him who was born according to the Spirit \\add [— Ishmael harassed Isaac, and the pattern repeats: the rival teachers, champions of the flesh-method, are now harassing you, the children of the promise-method]\\add*, so also it is now.
\\v 30 But what does the Scripture say? "Cast out the slave woman and her son, for the son of the slave woman shall not inherit with the son of the free woman"\\f + \\fr 4:30 \\ft Genesis 21:10.\\f* \\add [— listen to what the Torah itself commands: the flesh-method and its products are to be expelled. The very Scripture the rival teachers claim as their authority condemns their program]\\add*.
\\v 31 So, brothers, we are not children of the slave but of the free woman \\add [— this is the conclusion of the entire theological argument: our entry into Abraham's family was through promise and faith, not through circumcision and proselyte conversion; our mother is the Jerusalem above, not the present Jerusalem; our inheritance is secured by God's promise, not by human effort]\\add*.

\\c 5
\\s1 Stand Firm in Freedom
\\p
\\v 1 For freedom the Christ has set us free; stand firm therefore, and do not submit again to a yoke of slavery \\add [— "again" does not mean you were previously under the Torah; it means you would be returning to the same structural position of bondage to an external regulatory system, which is the category both your former paganism and the proposed proselyte conversion occupy]\\add*.
\\p
\\v 2 Look: I, Paul \\add [— I stake my personal apostolic authority on what follows —]\\add*, say to you that if you accept circumcision \\add [as the basis of your standing before God]\\add*, the Christ will be of no advantage to you \\add [— not because the Christ lacks power, but because you will have relocated to a track where his benefits do not operate]\\add*.
\\v 3 I testify again to every man who accepts circumcision that he is obligated to keep the whole law \\add [— circumcision is not one isolated requirement; it is the entry point into an entire covenant system that demands comprehensive obedience, and since no one achieves that, the result is not justification but the curse of Deuteronomy 27:26]\\add*.
\\v 4 You are severed from the Christ, you who would be justified by the law; you have fallen from grace \\add [— these are not threats of future punishment but descriptions of what relocating to the law-track inherently does: grace and law are mutually exclusive operating systems for justification; choosing one forfeits the other]\\add*.
\\v 5 For through the Spirit, by faith, we ourselves eagerly await the hope of righteousness \\add [— the entire trajectory, from initial justification to final vindication, operates by Spirit and faith; there is no point at which you switch from faith to law-performance]\\add*.
\\v 6 For in the Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision counts for anything, but only faith working through love\\f + \\fr 5:6 \\ft Compare 6:15: "neither circumcision nor uncircumcision, but a new creation."\\f* \\add [— the entire category of circumcision status is irrelevant; what matters is trust in God that expresses itself through self-giving love for one another]\\add*.

\\s1 The Agitators Exposed
\\p
\\v 7 You were running well \\add [— your faith was on course, your progress was real]\\add*. Who cut in on you \\add [— like someone cutting across a runner's lane to break their stride]\\add* to keep you from obeying the truth?
\\v 8 This persuasion is not from him who calls you \\add [— whatever arguments they use, whatever Scriptures they cite, their campaign does not originate from the God who called you into grace]\\add*.
\\v 9 A little leaven leavens the whole lump \\add [— even a small acceptance of the circumcision requirement will transform everything; the mutual exclusivity of grace and law means you cannot admit the law-system in any degree without compromising the gospel entirely]\\add*.
\\v 10 I have confidence in the Lord that you will take no other view, and the one who is troubling you will bear the penalty, whoever he is \\add [— I do not care about his credentials, his eloquence, or his reputation; only the content of his message matters, and his message is false]\\add*.
\\v 11 But if I, brothers, still preach circumcision \\add [— as they apparently claim I do]\\add*, why am I still being persecuted? In that case the offense of the cross has been removed \\add [— the cross is scandalous precisely because it claims to be sufficient without circumcision; if I preached circumcision, the scandal would disappear and the persecution would stop]\\add*.
\\v 12 I wish those who unsettle you would castrate themselves\\f + \\fr 5:12 \\ft See Deuteronomy 23:1.\\f* \\add [— if cutting flesh confers spiritual benefit, why stop at the foreskin? Let them go all the way — and in doing so, they would disqualify themselves from the very assembly they claim to guard, since the law of Moses excludes the castrated from God's congregation]\\add*!

\\s1 Freedom Expressed Through Love
\\p
\\v 13 For you were called to freedom, brothers. Only do not use your freedom as an opportunity for the flesh \\add [— freedom from the law's covenant jurisdiction is not permission to live without moral direction]\\add*, but through love serve one another \\add [— those freed from compulsory slavery are called to voluntary slavery: love-service to each other]\\add*.
\\v 14 For the whole law is fulfilled in one word: "You shall love your neighbor as yourself"\\f + \\fr 5:14 \\ft Leviticus 19:18. Compare Matthew 22:39-40; Romans 13:8-10.\\f* \\add [— this is a direct quotation from the Torah, Leviticus 19:18; I am teaching you Torah right now, and you are already keeping it. The rival teachers' demand is not "start keeping Torah" — you already are. Their demand is "become proselytes," which is an entirely different thing]\\add*.
\\v 15 But if you bite and devour one another \\add [— and this is exactly what the crisis over circumcision has produced among you: division, hostility, mutual destruction]\\add*, watch out that you are not consumed by one another.

\\s1 Walking by the Spirit
\\p
\\v 16 But I say, walk by the Spirit, and you will not gratify the desires of the flesh \\add [— this is a promise, not merely a command: the Spirit's power is sufficient; if you walk by the Spirit, the flesh's program will not be fulfilled]\\add*.
\\v 17 For the desires of the flesh are against the Spirit, and the desires of the Spirit are against the flesh, for these are opposed to each other, to keep you from doing the things you want to do \\add [— flesh and Spirit are warring powers with mutually exclusive aims; there is no neutral ground where both operate freely]\\add*.
\\v 18 But if you are led by the Spirit, you are not under the law \\add [— not because the Spirit-led are lawless, but because the Spirit produces from within what the law commanded from without, making the law's covenant-jurisdictional authority unnecessary for those in whom the Spirit is at work]\\add*.
\\p
\\v 19 Now the works of the flesh are evident \\add [— they are publicly recognizable, not hidden or ambiguous]\\add*: sexual immorality, impurity, sensuality,
\\v 20 idolatry, sorcery, enmity, strife, jealousy, fits of anger, rivalries, dissensions, divisions,
\\v 21 envy, drunkenness, orgies, and things like these \\add [— this list is representative, not exhaustive; notice how many of these are social sins that destroy community — enmity, strife, jealousy, rivalries, dissensions, divisions, envy — and this is exactly what the circumcision crisis is producing among you]\\add*. I warn you, as I warned you before, that those who practice such things will not inherit the kingdom of God \\add [— this warning is addressed to you, believers; the inheritance is real, but it is not automatically guaranteed regardless of how you live]\\add*.
\\p
\\v 22 But the fruit of the Spirit \\add [— notice: "fruit," singular, not "fruits" — the Spirit produces a single, unified harvest, not fragmented outputs]\\add* is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness,
\\v 23 gentleness, self-control; against such things there is no law \\add [— no legal system prohibits these qualities; the Spirit produces the very character the law aimed at, rendering the law's regulatory function unnecessary for those who walk by the Spirit]\\add*.
\\v 24 And those who belong to the Christ Jesus have crucified the flesh with its passions and desires \\add [— this already happened at your baptism; the flesh's ruling authority over you has been put to death; the ongoing conflict is real, but the verdict has already been rendered]\\add*.
\\v 25 If we live by the Spirit, let us also keep in step with the Spirit \\add [— if the Spirit is the source of our life, the Spirit must also be the director of our conduct]\\add*.
\\v 26 Let us not become conceited, provoking one another, envying one another \\add [— conceit, provocation, and envy are works of the flesh that destroy the very community the Spirit builds]\\add*.

\\c 6
\\s1 Bear One Another's Burdens
\\p
\\v 1 Brothers, if anyone is caught in any transgression \\add [— not habitual practice, but a stumble, an occasion where someone was overtaken before they could prevent it]\\add*, you who are spiritual \\add [i.e. you who are presently walking by the Spirit — this is not an elite rank but a description of those whose Spirit-produced character equips them for this task]\\add* should restore him in a spirit of gentleness \\add [— like mending a torn net or setting a broken bone; the goal is repair, not punishment]\\add*. Keep watch on yourself, lest you too be tempted \\add [— you are not immune; the one who restores stands on the same ground as the one who fell]\\add*.
\\v 2 Bear one another's burdens \\add [— the heavy loads that would crush individuals on their own]\\add*, and so fulfill the law of the Christ\\f + \\fr 6:2 \\ft Compare 1 Corinthians 9:21. The content is Leviticus 19:18, cited in 5:14.\\f* \\add [— this is the single command of neighbor-love from Leviticus 19:18, embodied by the Christ's own self-giving: "who loved me and gave himself for me." This is Torah content — the ethical heart of the Torah — operating through the Spirit rather than through covenant membership. You do not need circumcision to fulfill it; you are fulfilling it now]\\add*.
\\v 3 For if anyone thinks he is something, when he is nothing, he deceives himself \\add [— evaluating your standing by comparison with others, especially with the fallen, is self-delusion]\\add*.
\\v 4 But let each one test his own work, and then his reason to boast will be in himself alone and not in his neighbor \\add [— honest self-examination, not competitive comparison]\\add*.
\\v 5 For each one will have to bear his own load \\add [— this is not the same as the heavy burdens of verse 2; this is the personal responsibility each person carries before God, the tested work that cannot be transferred to someone else]\\add*.
\\p
\\v 6 Let the one who is taught the word share all good things with the one who teaches \\add [— material support for those who devote themselves to instruction; the relationship is reciprocal: they give spiritual nourishment, you give material provision]\\add*.

\\s1 You Reap What You Sow
\\p
\\v 7 Do not be deceived: God is not mocked \\add [— you cannot treat God's moral order with contempt and escape the consequences]\\add*, for whatever one sows, that will he also reap\\f + \\fr 6:7 \\ft Compare Hosea 8:7 and Job 4:8.\\f* \\add [— this is not mechanical karma but divinely established moral order: actions produce corresponding consequences, and there is no escape clause]\\add*.
\\v 8 For the one who sows to his own flesh \\add [— and this includes the circumcision program itself, since circumcision is literally an investment in physical flesh]\\add* will from the flesh reap corruption \\add [— decay, ruin, the opposite of life]\\add*, but the one who sows to the Spirit \\add [— every act of neighbor-love, every expression of the Spirit's fruit, every instance of mutual burden-bearing]\\add* will from the Spirit reap eternal life.
\\v 9 And let us not grow weary of doing good, for in due season we will reap, if we do not give up \\add [— the harvest is certain for those who persist, but seeds do not produce instant results; the delay does not mean the seed has failed]\\add*.
\\v 10 So then, as we have opportunity, let us do good to everyone, and especially to those who are of the household of faith \\add [— notice what defines the community: faith, not circumcision; the household is constituted by trust in the Christ, not by a mark on the body]\\add*.

\\s1 Paul's Own Hand: The Cross Against Circumcision
\\p
\\v 11 See with what large letters I am writing to you with my own hand \\add [— I have taken the pen from the secretary who has been writing at my dictation; everything that follows is in my own handwriting, for emphasis and authentication]\\add*.
\\v 12 It is those who want to make a good showing in the flesh \\add [i.e. to display circumcised converts as trophies of their success]\\add* who would force you to be circumcised, and only in order that they may not be persecuted for the cross of the Christ \\add [— their real motive is cowardice: by circumcising Gentile converts, they demonstrate to the wider Jewish community that the movement still respects the Sinai covenant's boundaries, and the pressure on them stops. They sacrifice your freedom to protect themselves]\\add*.
\\v 13 For even those who are circumcised do not themselves keep the law \\add [— the very thing they demand of you, they fail to accomplish themselves; they insist on the entry requirement while failing to meet the obligations that come with it]\\add*, but they desire to have you circumcised so that they may boast in your flesh \\add [— they want to use your bodies as badges of their own achievement]\\add*.
\\v 14 But far be it from me to boast except in the cross of our Lord Jesus the Christ, by which the world has been crucified to me, and I to the world \\add [— the entire system in which circumcision confers status, in which covenant membership determines worth, in which flesh-markers define identity — that world is dead to me, and I am dead to it; the cross executed it]\\add*.
\\v 15 For neither circumcision counts for anything, nor uncircumcision, but a new creation\\f + \\fr 6:15 \\ft Compare 2 Corinthians 5:17 and Isaiah 65:17.\\f* \\add [— this is the letter's definitive answer: what matters is not a mark on the body but a new act of God, on the scale of the original creation — the reality that the Spirit inhabits, that faith working through love expresses, and that replaces the present evil age from which the Christ has delivered us]\\add*.
\\v 16 And as for all who walk by this rule \\add [i.e. the standard of new creation rather than circumcision status]\\add*, peace and mercy be upon them, and upon the Israel of God\\f + \\fr 6:16 \\ft The phrase may refer to Jewish believers who walk by the new-creation standard, or to all believers redefined as God's Israel, distinguished from the "present Jerusalem" of 4:25.\\f* \\add [— God's own people, defined not by the flesh-method of the present Jerusalem but by the new-creation standard of the Jerusalem above]\\add*.
\\p
\\v 17 From now on let no one cause me trouble, for I bear on my body the marks of Jesus\\f + \\fr 6:17 \\ft Greek stigmata: marks branded on the body to identify a slave's owner. Paul's scars from persecution (2 Corinthians 11:23-25) are his brand of belonging to Jesus.\\f* \\add [— scars from beatings, stonings, and persecutions endured for the gospel; these are my marks of ownership, my brand of belonging — not the mark of circumcision, which the rival teachers want to put on your flesh, but the marks of suffering for the cross, which they are trying to avoid]\\add*.

\\s1 Final Blessing
\\p
\\v 18 The grace of our Lord Jesus the Christ be with your spirit, brothers \\add [— the letter that began with grace ends with grace; the word that has defined the gospel's sphere throughout this letter is my final prayer for you]\\add*. Amen.
`;
